Google Ads had the highest spend in 2023, at about $6.84M. The next highest were Meta at $1.57M, Display at $1.44M, CTV at $1.42M and LinkedIn at $1.29M.

All the weekly data starts on Mondays, so filtering on the year of the week start matches the house rule. I counted the five ad platforms as paid channels. Partner fees ($0.63M) are lower than Google either way.

{"answer": "Google", "refused": false}

Non-brand paid search cost per click in Q2 2024 was about $7.12. That is $1,160,557.13 of spend over 163,053 clicks.

I used the `FH_Search_NonBrand` campaign in `ads_google_weekly`. Q2 covers the 13 weeks whose Monday falls between 2024-04-01 and 2024-06-24.

{"answer": 7.12, "refused": false}

YouTube's CPM in 2023 was about $10.28. That is $765,109.20 of spend over 74,445,717 impressions, multiplied by 1,000.

I treated the Google Ads campaign `FH_YouTube_Prospecting` (campaign type VIDEO) as YouTube. It is the only YouTube campaign in the data. The figure covers 52 weeks, each assigned to 2023 by its Monday.

{"answer": 10.28, "refused": false}

Meta reported 55,659 leads on its own platform in 2025. That figure is my best match for "conversions", because the Meta table (`ads_meta_weekly`) has no column called conversions. Leads is the only conversion-type metric Meta reports there.

The total covers the weeks starting 2025-01-06 through 2025-12-22, which are the weeks whose Monday falls in 2025. The week starting 2024-12-30 is counted in 2024.

{"answer": 55659, "refused": false}

In 2024, 41,462 leads came from organic search. That is 36,538 from Google organic and 4,924 from Bing organic. I counted leads in `crm_leads` with `utm_medium` = organic and a created date in calendar 2024. Each lead ID appears once, so there are no duplicates.

I treated Bing organic as organic search. If you only want Google, the figure is 36,538.

{"answer": 41462, "refused": false}

Opportunities created in 2023 add up to $91,291,690 in pipeline. I counted every opportunity by its created date, whatever its final stage. That is $29,540,510 from Closed Won and $61,751,180 from Closed Lost, across 33,795 opportunities.

I had no stated definition of "pipeline created," so I used the standard one: the sum of `amount` on `crm_opportunities` by `created_date`. If you count only open or won opportunities, the number would be different.

